  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nVisible satellite at 11 AM was showing areas of high clouds across \nthe area with low clouds off the coast. There remain chances of \nisolated showers and thunderstorms in the mountains this afternoon. \nLatest REFS guidance indicates a decrease in the 90th percentile \nmaximum hourly rainfall rate this afternoon, down to 0.45 inches per \nhour. There is only a 10 percent chance of hourly rainfall rates \nexceeding that. Guidance continues to indicate 15-20 percent\nchance of isolated showers and thunderstorms over area mountains \nthrough Saturday, with guidance indicating hourly rainfall rates \nwill be less than 0.50 inch per hour. \n\nThrough Sunday high temperatures are expected to run 5 to 10 degrees \nabove average for inland areas. Low temperatures will also run above \naverage, most noticeable in the mountains and deserts where lows \nwill be 10 to 15 degrees above average. For the Inland Empire, \nmountains, and portions of inland Orange County and San Diego \nvalleys moderate HeatRisk is expected which means that folks who are \nsensitive heat, especially those without consistent \ncooling/hydration will be most impacted. In the deserts, major \nHeatRisk indicates anyone may see negative impacts from the heat if \nprecautions aren't taken. Make sure to stay hydrated and if possible \navoid strenuous activity during the hottest parts of the day (10 AM \nto 4 PM). High temperatures are expected to gradually decrease next \nweek, bringing temperatures back down to near or slightly below \naverage by the middle of next week. \n\nFor Sunday and Monday, precipitable water values are expected to \npeak which will result in more widespread shower and thunderstorm \nchances across the mountains. Shower chances will become more \nisolated again Tuesday and Wednesday.",
